\section{Principles}
\label{sec:principles}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,label=\textbf{P\arabic*.}]
\item \textbf{Finiteness.} \st{Postulate} The universe consists of a finite number $N$ of grains. Its Hilbert space is finite-dimensional. No physical quantity is infinite, either in the large or in the small.
\item \textbf{Grains are qubits.} \st{Postulate} Each grain carries a two-level quantum system. Its pure states form the Bloch sphere $\CP^1\cong S^2$, so the order parameter $\bn$ of V2/V3 is the Bloch vector of a grain.
\item \textbf{Space is a pattern.} \st{Postulate} Which grains are neighbours is a dynamical quantum variable. Distance, dimension and locality are properties of particular states of the network, not background structure.
\item \textbf{Time is relational.} \st{Postulate} There is no external time. Physical states obey a global constraint, and time is defined by correlations between a clock subsystem and the rest.
\item \textbf{Emergence.} \st{Postulate} Photons, fermions, gravity and Lorentz symmetry are low-energy collective properties of the geometric phase.
\item \textbf{Cycles without a master clock.} \st{Conjecture} Cosmic history is a sequence of transitions between pre-geometric and geometric phases. Since no clock spans the transitions, ``how many cycles'' has no physical answer.
\end{enumerate}

