\section{Kinematics: the grain network}
\label{sec:kinematics}

\paragraph{Nodes.} Grains $i=1,\dots,N$ each carry $\Hc_i\cong\mathbb C^2$. Equivalently, using Schwinger bosons $b_{i\sigma}$ ($\sigma=\uparrow,\downarrow$) with $\sum_\sigma b^\dagger_{i\sigma}b_{i\sigma}=1$, the Bloch vector is $\bn_i=b_i^\dagger\boldsymbol\sigma b_i$. The phase of $b_i$ is a $U(1)$ redundancy. This is the lattice origin of the composite connection $a_\mu=-iz^\dagger\partial_\mu z$ of V3.

\paragraph{Links.} Every pair $(ij)$ of the complete graph $K_N$ carries a finite-dimensional link space
\begin{equation}
\Hc_{ij} = \mathrm{span}\{\ket{\mathrm{off}}\}\ \oplus\ \big(\ket{\mathrm{on}}\otimes\mathbb C^{2S+1}\big).
\end{equation}
An \emph{on} link joins neighbours and carries a quantum-link spin $\boldsymbol S_{ij}$. Its electric flux is $E_{ij}=S^z_{ij}$ and its parallel transporter is $U_{ij}=S^+_{ij}$. This is the quantum-link formulation of $U(1)$ gauge theory, which needs only finite-dimensional link spaces~\cite{ChandrasekharanWiese1997,Wiese2013}. \st{Established} The full Hilbert space $\Hc=\bigotimes_i\Hc_i\otimes\bigotimes_{i<j}\Hc_{ij}$ is finite-dimensional, as P1 requires.

\paragraph{Emergent geometry.} In a state whose on-links form a graph $\Gamma$, the distance $d(i,j)$ is the graph distance in units of the grain size $\ell$. Effective dimension is read from ball growth ($|B_r|\sim r^{d_H}$) or from the spectral dimension. ``Space is three-dimensional'' means the geometric phase has $d_H\simeq3$ on scales $\gg\ell$.

