\section{Dynamics: the timeless constraint}
\label{sec:dynamics}

\subsection{The constraint operator}
Physical states satisfy
\begin{equation}
\hat G_i\ket\Psi = 0\ \ (\text{all } i), \qquad \hat H\ket\Psi = 0,
\label{eq:constraints}
\end{equation}
with Gauss operators $\hat G_i=\sum_j E_{ij}-\hat Q_i$ ($\hat Q_i$ the node charge) and
\begin{equation}
\hat H = \hat H_{V} + \hat H_{L} + \hat H_{q} + \hat H_{g}.
\end{equation}
Schematically, in the spirit of quantum graphity~\cite{KMS2006,KMS2008}:
\begin{align}
\hat H_V &= g_V\sum_i(\hat v_i-v_0)^2, \qquad \hat v_i=\sum_j P^{\rm on}_{ij}
&&\text{(valence: each grain prefers $v_0$ neighbours)},\\
\hat H_L &= -g_L\sum_{\gamma:\,|\gamma|\le L_0} r^{|\gamma|}\prod_{e\in\gamma}P^{\rm on}_e
&&\text{(favours short closed loops: lattice-like order)},\\
\hat H_q &= -t\sum_{(ij)\,\rm on}\sum_\sigma\big(b^\dagger_{i\sigma}U_{ij}b_{j\sigma}+\mathrm{h.c.}\big)
&&\text{(spinon hopping, gauge covariant)},\\
\hat H_g &= \frac{g^2}{2}\sum_{(ij)\,\rm on}E_{ij}^2-\frac{1}{2g^2}\sum_{\square}\big(U_\square+U_\square^\dagger\big)
&&\text{(quantum-link gauge dynamics)}.
\end{align}
All terms are bounded operators on a finite-dimensional space. The specific forms are illustrative: V4 commits to the \emph{ingredients} (valence, loop, matter and gauge terms), not yet to unique coefficients. \st{Open}

\subsection{Relational time}
Partition the network into a clock $C$ and the rest $R$, with $\hat H\simeq\hat H_C\otimes\mathbb 1+\mathbb 1\otimes\hat H_R$. Following Page and Wootters~\cite{PageWootters1983}, a solution of $\hat H\ket\Psi=0$ can be written $\ket\Psi=\sum_\tau\ket{\tau}_C\ket{\psi(\tau)}_R$, and the conditional states obey
\begin{equation}
i\,\frac{d}{d\tau}\ket{\psi(\tau)}_R \simeq \hat H_R\ket{\psi(\tau)}_R .
\end{equation}
\st{Established} for idealized clocks~\cite{PageWootters1983,HohnSmithLock2021}. It has been demonstrated in a photonic experiment~\cite{Moreva2014}.

Because $C$ is finite, $\tau$ takes discrete values and the Schr\"odinger evolution is approximate. This matches P1: time is grainy too, and continuous time is an idealization.

\subsection{The arrow of time}
Define the coarse-grained entropy $S_{\rm cg}(\tau)$ of $\ket{\psi(\tau)}_R$ relative to a coarse-graining set by the emergent geometry. The arrow of time is the direction of increasing $S_{\rm cg}$. \st{Conjecture} The geometrogenesis transition (Section~\ref{sec:cosmo}) is a \emph{Janus point}~\cite{Barbour2020,CarrollChen2004}: a configuration of minimal structure on either side of which $S_{\rm cg}$ grows. Observers on both sides then experience it as their past. No special low-entropy initial condition is postulated.

