\section{Gravity from grain entanglement}
\label{sec:gravity}

\paragraph{Area law.} In the geometric phase, the entanglement entropy of the grains across a surface $\Sigma$ of area $|\Sigma|$ obeys
\begin{equation}
S_{\rm ent}(\Sigma) = \eta_g\,\frac{|\Sigma|}{\ell^2}+\cdots,
\end{equation}
with $\eta_g=O(1)$ counting the entangled degrees of freedom per unit area.

\paragraph{Einstein's equations as an equation of state.} \st{Established} If $S=|\Sigma|/4G$ holds for all local causal horizons and the Clausius relation $\delta Q=T\delta S$ holds, Einstein's equations follow~\cite{Jacobson1995}. An equivalent route uses entanglement equilibrium~\cite{Jacobson2016}. In CCEF this gives
\begin{equation}
G = \frac{\ell^2}{4\eta_g}.
\label{eq:G}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Consistency with induced gravity.} \st{Established} The entanglement-entropy coefficient and Sakharov's induced $1/G$ arise from the same sum over species~\cite{Jacobson1994,SusskindUglum1994}. Matching eq.~\eqref{eq:G} to V3's $1/16\pi G=N_{\rm eff}M^2/96\pi^2$ with cutoff $M=1/\ell$ gives
\begin{equation}
\eta_g = \frac{N_{\rm eff}}{24\pi}\quad(\approx0.12 \text{ for } N_{\rm eff}=9),
\qquad
\ell = 2\sqrt{\eta_g}\;\lP \approx 1.1\times10^{-35}\ \text{m},
\end{equation}
with $1/\ell\approx1.77\times10^{19}$\,GeV. That reproduces V3's induced-gravity scale exactly. The grain size is therefore \emph{fixed by Newton's constant}, not chosen freely.

\paragraph{The graviton.} The Weinberg--Witten theorem assumes exact Lorentz invariance~\cite{WeinbergWitten1980}. In V4 Lorentz symmetry is emergent, so a graviton built from the network is no longer excluded. Lattice models with emergent gravitons exist at the linearized level~\cite{GuWen2006,Xu2006}. Whether CCEF's geometric phase supports a propagating massless spin-2 mode, beyond the thermodynamic derivation of Einstein's equations, is \st{Open}.

