\section{Dark energy from finiteness}
\label{sec:lambda}

\paragraph{Mechanism.} \st{Conjecture, following causal-set theory~\cite{ADGS2004,Sorkin2007}} Take the 4-volume of a region to be its grain count, $V_4=N\ell^4$. This is a unimodular-type condition, under which a constant vacuum energy does not gravitate, so the $10^{122}$ problem of V3 is removed. Treat $\Lambda$ as conjugate to $V_4$. If $N$ fluctuates by $\Delta N\sim\sqrt N$, then
\begin{equation}
\rho_\Lambda\sim\frac{1}{\Delta V_4}\sim\frac{1}{\sqrt N\,\ell^4}.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Magnitude.} For the past 4-volume of the observable universe, $V_4\sim H_0^{-4}$, so $N\sim(H_0\lP)^{-4}\sim10^{244}$ and
\begin{equation}
\Lambda\,\lP^2\sim N^{-1/2}\sim10^{-122}\quad(\text{observed: }2.9\times10^{-122}),
\qquad
\rho_\Lambda\sim H^2\Mpl^2\quad(\text{observed: }\rho_\Lambda\approx2.1\,H_0^2\Mpl^2).
\end{equation}
Sorkin made this estimate before dark energy was discovered.

\paragraph{Consequences.}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item $\rho_\Lambda$ tracks $H^2$ at every epoch. This addresses the ``why now?'' coincidence problem.
\item $\rho_\Lambda$ fluctuates stochastically and can change sign. Dark energy is \emph{not} exactly constant, which is relevant to the DESI indications of evolving dark energy.
\item \textbf{Requirement:} the mechanism needs genuine number fluctuations. It favours the dynamical, partly random connectivity of Section~\ref{sec:kinematics} over a rigid crystal.
\item \textbf{Status:} phenomenological versions face constraints from the CMB and big-bang nucleosynthesis, but viable parameter ranges have been reported~\cite{ZwaneAfshordiSorkin2018}. \st{Open} Derive the fluctuation law from $\hat H$.
\end{itemize}

