\section{Cosmology}
\label{sec:cosmo}

\subsection{Geometrogenesis as the Big Bang}
\st{Conjecture} The hot Big Bang is the transition from the pre-geometric to the geometric phase.

\paragraph{Horizon problem.} \st{Established as a mechanism~\cite{KMS2008,MSC2007}} In the pre-geometric phase every grain is adjacent to every other, so the whole network equilibrates. Regions that later lie far apart in the emergent geometry inherit a common temperature, without inflation.

\paragraph{Flatness.} \st{Conjecture} A lattice-like geometric phase is flat on scales $\gg\ell$ unless defects impose curvature. Large-scale flatness is then the default outcome of crystallization rather than fine-tuning.

\subsection{Primordial perturbations}
\st{Conjecture} If the transition passes near a critical point, primordial perturbations are critical fluctuations, and the scalar tilt is controlled by a critical exponent, $n_s=1-\eta$. Thermal-origin scenarios with near scale invariance have precedent~\cite{MSC2007,NBV2006}.

\paragraph{Prior art.} The relation $n_s\simeq1-\eta$ with the 3D Ising exponent was proposed earlier by Irges, Kalogirou and Koutroulis~\cite{IKK2023a,IKK2023b}. They argue via dS/CFT that the power spectrum tracks the inverse of the boundary stress-tensor-trace correlator, whose normalization runs with eigenvalue $\eta$. They describe their supporting steps as plausibility arguments. The more general idea that a tilt reflects an anomalous scaling dimension goes back to Antoniadis, Mazur and Mottola~\cite{AMM1997}. A rival parameter-free prediction, $n_s=0.96478$ with running $-\tfrac32(n_s-1)^2$ and $r=0$, comes from Afshordi and Magueijo's critical thermal Big Bang~\cite{AM2016}. CCEF's contribution here is limited to placing the relation at a geometrogenesis transition rather than in a de Sitter background, and to the analysis below.

\paragraph{What the relation requires.} If the curvature spectrum were the critical order parameter itself, then $\Delta^2_\zeta\propto k^3G(k)\propto k^{1+\eta}$, which is strongly blue. A local energy density is also blue. In induced gravity, however, geometry is a \emph{source} for the grain system, so its effective action is $\tfrac12\int\zeta K\zeta$ with $K$ a grain correlator, and $P_\zeta=1/K$. This inverse relation is the same structure as in dS/CFT and holographic cosmology. If $K$ is the correlator of an operator of dimension $\Delta$ at a 3D fixed point, then
\begin{equation}
n_s-1 = 6-2\Delta .
\end{equation}
The trace $\Theta$ has $\Delta=3$ exactly, giving $n_s=1$. The simplest estimate for the gradient (stiffness) energy, $\Delta=3+\eta$, gives $n_s=1-2\eta\approx0.927$, excluded. The value $n_s=1-\eta$ would need $\Delta=3+\eta/2$, and no such operator exists in the Ising or $O(3)$ spectrum. \st{Open} The relation can therefore only arise from near-critical drift of $K(k)$. There the natural exponents are $\nu$ and $\omega$, not $\eta$, and the tilt generically runs. Whether $1-\eta$ survives a first-principles treatment is the decisive calculation. A negative answer would also bear on~\cite{IKK2023a}.

\paragraph{Running constraint.} \st{Result of this work} A drift with exponent $y$ gives $n_s-1\propto k^{\pm y}$, and hence
\begin{equation}
\alpha_s\equiv\frac{dn_s}{d\ln k}=\pm\,y\,(n_s-1).
\end{equation}
For the Ising class at $n_s-1=-0.035$:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item relevant drift ($y=1/\nu$) gives $\alpha_s=+0.056$, excluded at about $9\sigma$;
\item irrelevant drift ($y=\omega$) gives $\alpha_s=-0.029$, disfavoured at about $3.7\sigma$~\cite{Planck2018}.
\end{itemize}
A lattice measurement of effective dimensions at and near $K_c$ (\texttt{ns\_drift\_test.py}, $L=32$) confirms the assumed drift. It finds no scale window with $n_s\approx0.965$ and Planck-compatible running. A viable critical origin therefore requires a nearly marginal operator, for example cubic anisotropy in the $O(3)$ class with $y\approx0.01$. In that case $n_s$ becomes a fitted amplitude rather than a parameter-free prediction.

\paragraph{Tension: mean-field geometrogenesis.} Landau--Ginzburg analyses of group-field-theory condensation, the closest worked-out model of geometrogenesis, find the Ginzburg criterion satisfied, so mean-field theory holds~\cite{MOPT2023}. Mean-field behaviour means $\eta=0$ and hence $n_s=1$ exactly, which Planck excludes at about $8\sigma$. CCEF therefore \emph{requires} its geometrogenesis transition to lie in a non-mean-field class with $\eta\approx0.035$. This is a sharp requirement on $\hat H$ that could rule the idea out.

