\paragraph{Tensor modes.} \st{Open} By the same inverse relation, tensors follow the inverse of the transverse--traceless part of $\langle TT\rangle$. Its dimension is protected, so $n_t=0$ at the fixed point, and any tilt comes from the same near-critical drift as $n_s$. The sign is not yet determined. String-gas cosmology predicts a blue tilt~\cite{NBV2006}, while conformal-invariance arguments give $n_T=n_s-1$~\cite{AMM1997}. Two firm discriminators remain. Afshordi--Magueijo predicts $r=0$~\cite{AM2016}, whereas V4 allows $r>0$. Single-field inflation obeys $n_t=-r/8$.

\subsection{Cycles}
\paragraph{A minimal, model-independent cyclicity.} \st{Established for finite systems~\cite{BocchieriLoinger1957}} A finite quantum system returns arbitrarily close to any earlier state. Under P1, the universe therefore revisits pre-geometric configurations.

\paragraph{Cycle interpretation.} \st{Conjecture} The geometric phase eventually reaches maximal coarse-grained entropy, loses usable structure, and re-melts into the pre-geometric phase. That phase is again a Janus point and the start of a new geometric phase. With time defined only by internal clocks, which do not survive the pre-geometric phase, no clock counts cycles. ``Infinitely many cycles'' is therefore not a physical statement, consistent with P1. A dynamical re-melting mechanism faster than Poincar\'e recurrence is \st{Open}.

\subsection{The late, lumpy universe}
\st{Conjecture} Because time is relational and local, clocks in under-dense voids and in bound structures accumulate different proper times. Cosmological observables should be computed by averaging over this inhomogeneous structure~\cite{Buchert2000} rather than from a single global FLRW clock. A timescape-type skew in supernova distances~\cite{Wiltshire2007,Seifert2024} then follows from P4 instead of being an add-on.

